    <statement><p>Use <m>\cos(t)</m> and <m>\sin(t)</m>, with positive coefficients, to parametrize the intersection of the surfaces <m>x^2+y^2=4</m> and <m>z=6 x^{2}</m>.</p>
<p><m>\bf r\it (t)=\left\lt  \right.</m>
  <fillin characters="20" name="AnSwEr0001"/>,
  <fillin characters="20" name="MuLtIaNsWeR_AnSwEr0001_1"/>,
  <fillin characters="20" name="MuLtIaNsWeR_AnSwEr0001_2"/>
<m>\left. \right&gt;</m></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>2\cos\mathopen{}\left(t\right);\,2\sin\mathopen{}\left(t\right);\,6\mathopen{}\left(2\cos\mathopen{}\left(t\right)\right)^{2}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>x^2+y^2=4</m> and on <m>z=6 x^{2}</m> can be written in the form: 


<m>x^2+y^2=4 \rightarrow \left(2 \cos t, 2 \sin t, z\right)</m> 


<m>z=6 x^{2} \rightarrow \left(x,y,6 x^{2} \right)</m> 


The points <m>(x,y,z)</m> on the intersection curve must satisfy the following equations:



<m>x=2\cos t</m>


<m>y=2\sin t</m> 


<m>z=6 x^{2}=6 (2 \cos t)^{2}</m> 


We obtain the vector parametrization: 


<m>\bf r\it (t)=\left\lt  2 \cos t, 2 \sin t, 6(2 \cos t)^{2} \right&gt;</m>





</solution>
